---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Calculate Battery Runtime';
const description = 'Battery runtime = usable capacity divided by the load. Worked examples — Ah ÷ amps, Wh ÷ watts — with the Peukert effect caveat that higher loads deliver less.';
const canonical = '/lead-acid/runtime-calculation/';

const articleJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}#article`,
  headline: title,
  url: `https://worldbatteryhub.com${canonical}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  about: 'Battery runtime calculation',
  articleSection: 'Lead-Acid Battery Knowledge',
  dateModified: '2026-09-16',
  isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/#website' },
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How do you calculate how long a battery will last?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Divide the usable capacity by the load: hours = Ah ÷ amps (or Wh ÷ watts). A 100 Ah battery at a 10 A load runs roughly 10 hours to empty — about 5 hours if you limit lead-acid discharge to 50% for longevity.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Why does a battery deliver less capacity at high loads?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'The Peukert effect: internal losses grow with current, so the same battery delivers fewer usable amp-hours at a fast discharge than at the slow 20-hour rate its capacity is rated at. The nameplate Ah assumes the C20 rate.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Practical · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>How to Calculate Battery Runtime</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Every battery runtime question is one division — capacity over load — with two honest adjustments on top:
        how deep you may discharge, and how fast the load drains reality beyond the nameplate.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Runtime in hours = <strong>usable capacity ÷ load</strong>:
        <strong>hours = Ah ÷ amps</strong> (or <strong>Wh ÷ watts</strong>). A 100 Ah battery at a 10 A load runs
        about <strong>10 hours to empty</strong> — roughly <strong>5 hours</strong> if you limit lead-acid
        discharge to 50% for longevity (see <a href="/lead-acid/deep-cycle-vs-starting/">deep cycling</a>).
      </div>

      <h2>How to calculate, step by step</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pick the capacity unit that matches your load</strong> — Ah with amps, or Wh with watts.</li>
        <li><strong>Find the usable capacity</strong> — apply the depth-of-discharge window: ~50% of rated Ah for lead-acid, 80–90% for LFP.</li>
        <li><strong>Divide capacity by load</strong> — hours = Ah ÷ amps (or Wh ÷ watts).</li>
        <li><strong>Adjust for the load's rate</strong> — for heavy loads near 1C, the Peukert effect means the battery delivers less than the C20 nameplate.</li>
        <li><strong>State the answer with its assumptions</strong> — which DoD and which discharge rate the runtime figure assumes.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Worked examples</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Battery</th><th>Load</th><th>Calculation</th><th>Runtime</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>100 Ah (C20)</td><td>10 A</td><td>100 ÷ 10</td><td>~10 h to empty (~5 h at 50% DoD)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100 Ah (C20)</td><td>5 A</td><td>100 ÷ 5</td><td>~20 h to empty</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,200 Wh (lithium)</td><td>120 W</td><td>1,200 ÷ 120</td><td>~10 h (usable at 80–90% DoD)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The Peukert adjustment</h2>
      <p>
        The nameplate Ah assumes the slow <strong>C20 rate</strong> (see
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/capacity-rates/">C20/C10/C5</a>). At higher currents the
        internal losses grow, so the same battery delivers <em>fewer</em> usable amp-hours — a 100 Ah battery
        drained at 50 A may deliver far less than 2 hours. For loads near or above 1C, check the rate-capacity
        data instead of trusting the division.
      </p>

      <h2>The depth-of-discharge decision</h2>
      <p>
        The second adjustment is the <strong>usable window</strong>: lead-acid survives longest at 50% depth of
        discharge (so plan half the rated Ah), while lithium LFP allows 80–90% (see
        <a href="/lithium/lifespan/">cycle life vs depth</a>). The runtime formula stays the same — the
        difference is which fraction of the capacity you count as usable.
      </p>
      <h2>The three common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Using the full nameplate Ah</strong> — lead-acid's usable window is ~50%, LFP's 80–90%</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring the load's rate</strong> — heavy loads deliver less than the C20 nameplate (Peukert)</li>
        <li><strong>Mixing units</strong> — Ah needs amps, Wh needs watts; converting at the wrong voltage breaks the result</li>
      </ul>


      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My take:</strong> Runtime calculation is one honest division plus two honest adjustments — and
          most planning errors are the adjustments, not the arithmetic: people divide by the nameplate Ah and
          forget the load's Peukert bite and the depth-of-discharge limit.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why:</strong> The formula is trivial; the usable-window discipline is everything. A plan built on
          usable capacity — half the rating for lead-acid, 80–90% for LFP, rate-adjusted for heavy loads — is
          rarely off by much, and it is the difference between a system that matches its math and one that
          disappoints its owner.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>At which rate (C20/C10/C5) is the capacity figure rated?</li>
        <li>What depth of discharge does the application allow?</li>
        <li>What is the actual load in amps or watts, including peaks?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>Runtime is capacity over load, corrected for the depth you may discharge and the Peukert effect at higher loads. Run the two adjustments or the estimate overshoots reality — and in a backup or off-grid case, that overshoot is the failure.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from battery engineering references and manufacturer discharge data.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery engineering references — Peukert behaviour and rate capacity.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer datasheets — capacity ratings at standard rates.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Prefer the interactive version? <a href="/tools/runtime-calculator/">Use the calculator</a>. Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
